{"version_id": 51, "article_id": 6, "level_code": "en_a1", "lang": "en", "level": 1, "level_label": "A1 入门", "title": "Science needs fair rules in a world of conflict", "title_zh": "世界有冲突，科学合作需要更公平的规则", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 158, "reading_minutes": 0.8, "lead": "Science is a way for countries to work together. But today, politics makes this hard. Two writers say we need fairer rules.", "lead_zh": "科学是各国合作的一种方式。但如今，政治让合作变得困难。两位作者说，我们需要更公平的规则。", "preview": "Science is often a bridge between countries. Many people think science is above politics. But this is changing now. Countries around the wor", "preview_zh": "科学常常是国家之间的桥梁。很多人认为科学高于政治。但现在情况在变化。今天，世界各国之间的紧张关系更多了。这种紧张也影响到了科学。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 41, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/6/en_a1.mp3", "duration": 75.562, "size_bytes": 457056, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 183, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/51.json", "paragraphs": ["Science is often a bridge between countries. Many people think science is above politics. But this is changing now. Countries around the world have more tensions today. These tensions affect science too.", "The writers are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. They work at universities in China. Edeh works at Shanghai University. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University. They wrote about this problem in Nature.", "They say one big problem is affiliation. Affiliation means the university or group you work for. Today, people look at your affiliation. Then they think about your country. Your university can become like a political label.", "The writers say this is not fair. Good scientists can lose chances to work together. Science becomes slower for everyone. The writers want fairer rules. They say science should stay open to all.", "Their letter came out on 6 October 2026. It is in Nature, volume 658. The writers say they have no competing interests."], "paragraphs_zh": ["科学常常是国家之间的桥梁。很多人认为科学高于政治。但现在情况在变化。今天，世界各国之间的紧张关系更多了。这种紧张也影响到了科学。", "两位作者是 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们在中国的大学工作。Edeh 在上海大学工作。Omoruyi 在浙江师范大学工作。他们在《自然》杂志上写了这个问题。", "他们说一个大问题是“所属机构”。所属机构就是你工作的大学或团队。今天，人们会看你的所属机构，然后想到你的国家。你的大学可能变成一个政治标签。", "两位作者说，这不公平。优秀的科学家可能失去合作的机会。这样一来，所有人的科学研究都会变慢。作者们想要更公平的规则。他们说，科学应该对所有人保持开放。", "他们的来信发表于 2026 年 10 月 6 日。文章发表在《自然》杂志第 658 卷。作者们表示没有利益冲突。"], "body": "Science is often a bridge between countries. Many people think science is above politics. But this is changing now. Countries around the world have more tensions today. These tensions affect science too.\n\nThe writers are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. They work at universities in China. Edeh works at Shanghai University. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University. They wrote about this problem in Nature.\n\nThey say one big problem is affiliation. Affiliation means the university or group you work for. Today, people look at your affiliation. Then they think about your country. Your university can become like a political label.\n\nThe writers say this is not fair. Good scientists can lose chances to work together. Science becomes slower for everyone. The writers want fairer rules. They say science should stay open to all.\n\nTheir letter came out on 6 October 2026. It is in Nature, volume 658. The writers say they have no competing interests.", "body_zh": "科学常常是国家之间的桥梁。很多人认为科学高于政治。但现在情况在变化。今天，世界各国之间的紧张关系更多了。这种紧张也影响到了科学。\n\n两位作者是 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh 和 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们在中国的大学工作。Edeh 在上海大学工作。Omoruyi 在浙江师范大学工作。他们在《自然》杂志上写了这个问题。\n\n他们说一个大问题是“所属机构”。所属机构就是你工作的大学或团队。今天，人们会看你的所属机构，然后想到你的国家。你的大学可能变成一个政治标签。\n\n两位作者说，这不公平。优秀的科学家可能失去合作的机会。这样一来，所有人的科学研究都会变慢。作者们想要更公平的规则。他们说，科学应该对所有人保持开放。\n\n他们的来信发表于 2026 年 10 月 6 日。文章发表在《自然》杂志第 658 卷。作者们表示没有利益冲突。", "text": "Science is often a bridge between countries. Many people think science is above politics. But this is changing now. Countries around the world have more tensions today. These tensions affect science too. The writers are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi. They work at universities in China. Edeh works at Shanghai University. Omoruyi works at Zhejiang Normal University. They wrote about this problem in Nature. They say one big problem is affiliation. Affiliation means the university or group you work for. Today, people look at your affiliation. Then they think about your country. Your university can become like a political label. The writers say this is not fair. Good scientists can lose chances to work together. Science becomes slower for everyone. The writers want fairer rules. They say science should stay open to all. Their letter came out on 6 October 2026. It is in Nature, volume 658. The writers say they have no competing interests.", "vocab": [{"word": "science", "pos": "noun", "zh": "科学", "note": "常见基础词"}, {"word": "country", "pos": "noun", "zh": "国家", "note": "复数 countries"}, {"word": "work", "pos": "verb", "zh": "工作", "note": "也可作名词"}, {"word": "university", "pos": "noun", "zh": "大学", "note": "常见地点词"}, {"word": "problem", "pos": "noun", "zh": "问题", "note": "常用词"}, {"word": "fair", "pos": "adj", "zh": "公平的", "note": "反义词 unfair"}, {"word": "rule", "pos": "noun", "zh": "规则", "note": "常用复数 rules"}, {"word": "open", "pos": "adj", "zh": "开放的", "note": "也可作动词"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any time\nLearn more\nSubscribe to this journal\nReceive 52 print issues and online access\n$199.00 per year\nonly $3.83 per issue\nLearn more\nRent or buy this article\nPrices vary by article type\nfrom\n$1.95\nto\n$39.95\nLearn more\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\nNature\n658\n, 572 (2026)\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nCompeting Interests\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\nRelated Articles\nAcademic boycotts: data hint at shifting research partnerships\nGeopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink research collaboration\nGlobal geopolitics should not stall science — 5 ways to push back\nSubjects\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nLatest on:\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nHow to mobilize the agile science needed in a crisis\nEditorial\n06 OCT 26\nRising numbers of Asian and African PhD students choose to study in China